$E$ from the left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134190 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67780f7b-b335-51d6-8512-e61d1ae7ee4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134190 #edb/ref 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by swapping the first and the second rows of ${I}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134602 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134602 #edb/ref 1061 17243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa6e7f35-addb-57cf-a360-9e6406254399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134603 #edb/ref 1061 17244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fd97122-b858-5040-a119-fad68a3d03fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134604 #edb/ref 1061 17245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da013532-9009-5401-a121-9e5bc3920d43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134605 #edb/ref 1061 17246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec9418ab-c448-5510-808b-5f8b1a2094a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135024 #edb/ref 1064 "Applying a row operation to any $3\\times 3$ matrix is the same as *pre-multiplying* (multiplying on the left) the matrix by the corresponding elementary matrix.\n\nFor instance, if we wish to swap the first row and the second row of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ we pre-multiply $A$ by the corresponding elementary matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nto get $E_1 A.$\n\nTo perform a sequence of row operations to a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we pre-multiply the matrix by the elementary matrix corresponding to the first operation, and then pre-multiply the result by the elementary matrix corresponding to the second operation.\n\nFor instance, given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ suppose that we would like to multiply the second row by $2$ and then add $3$ times the first row to the second row. To do this, we first pre-multiply $A$ by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nto get $E_2 A.$ Then we pre-multiply the result by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nto get $E_3 E_2 A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135024 #edb/ref 1065 3171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135024 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c110a2ca-8856-5b26-9ded-2e9f35023747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135024 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244336 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244336 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244336 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03125329-a0ee-59fd-b6ae-a6f2a29a7ae5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244374 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244374 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045